::ENG4_U04_UA_Q01::
Question ID: ENG4_U04_UA_Q01
[MLA.ENG4.RS.01] Sources show senior transition workshops help when students use realistic examples, receive feedback, and revise. Which synthesis is strongest?
{
~A. Transition workshops exist in schools.#Incorrect. This does not synthesize source relationships.
=B. Transition workshops are most useful when they combine realistic tasks, feedback, and revision time.#Correct. This connects all source ideas into one synthesis.
~C. Feedback is unrelated to student preparation.#Incorrect. Feedback is central in the source set.
~D. Students should avoid revision.#Incorrect. This contradicts the source set.
}

::ENG4_U04_UA_Q02::
Question ID: ENG4_U04_UA_Q02
[MLA.ENG4.W.02] Which claim best follows from the transition workshop source set?
{
~A. Schools should end all senior support.#Incorrect. This ignores the evidence.
~B. Workshops are perfect in every situation.#Incorrect. This overstates the evidence.
~C. Students never need examples.#Incorrect. The sources say realistic examples help.
=D. Schools should provide senior transition workshops that include realistic tasks and revision after feedback.#Correct. This is specific, arguable, and evidence-based.
}

::ENG4_U04_UA_Q03::
Question ID: ENG4_U04_UA_Q03
[MLA.ENG4.W.03] Passage detail: marked drafts and an open door remain after a workshop. Which analysis is strongest?
{
=A. The details present revision as an ongoing process of preparation and growth.#Correct. This explains function and meaning.
~B. The room has papers.#Incorrect. This summarizes a detail.
~C. The projector is off.#Incorrect. This identifies a detail without explaining meaning.
~D. The workshop happened indoors.#Incorrect. This is literal, not analytical.
}

::ENG4_U04_UA_Q04::
Question ID: ENG4_U04_UA_Q04
[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Source note: Students revised materials after feedback. Which paraphrase is accurate?
{
~A. Students refused all feedback.#Incorrect. This reverses the source.
~B. Students only listened to lectures.#Incorrect. This changes the source.
=C. Students improved their materials after receiving responses from others.#Correct. This preserves the meaning in new wording.
~D. Students never revised their work.#Incorrect. This contradicts the source.
}

::ENG4_U04_UA_Q05::
Question ID: ENG4_U04_UA_Q05
[MLA.ENG4.W.04] Which revision improves "The sources are good"?
{
~A. The sources are good good good.#Incorrect. Repetition does not improve writing.
~B. The sources say things.#Incorrect. This remains vague.
=C. The sources show that workshops are strongest when students practice realistic tasks and revise after feedback.#Correct. This revision adds precise meaning.
~D. Good sources are good.#Incorrect. This is circular.
}

::ENG4_U04_UA_Q06::
Question ID: ENG4_U04_UA_Q06
[MLA.ENG4.L.01] Which sentence is clearest?
{
=A. Workshops can support seniors when they include realistic examples, feedback, and revision time.#Correct. This is clear and grammatically complete.
~B. Workshops because students and tasks and feedback.#Incorrect. This is fragmented.
~C. The workshop, feedback, revisions, because students.#Incorrect. This is unclear.
~D. Students things for after graduation.#Incorrect. This is vague and incomplete.
}

::ENG4_U04_UA_Q07::
Question ID: ENG4_U04_UA_Q07
[MLA.ENG4.W.03] Which response best fits an academic checkpoint?
{
~A. Use slang and omit evidence.#Incorrect. This does not fit the audience or task.
~B. Submit only a title.#Incorrect. This does not communicate mastery.
~C. Avoid explaining the response.#Incorrect. Explanation is required.
=D. State the claim, use evidence, and explain reasoning in a professional tone.#Correct. This matches academic communication expectations.
}

::ENG4_U04_UA_Q08::
Question ID: ENG4_U04_UA_Q08
[MLA.ENG4.RS.01] Sources show a policy has benefits but requires staffing. Which synthesis is strongest?
{
~A. Staffing concerns mean the policy has no benefits.#Incorrect. This ignores benefits.
=B. The policy may be useful when staffing needs are planned before implementation.#Correct. This connects benefit and condition.
~C. Benefits mean staffing never matters.#Incorrect. This ignores the concern.
~D. The sources are unrelated.#Incorrect. They address the same policy.
}

::ENG4_U04_UA_Q09::
Question ID: ENG4_U04_UA_Q09
[MLA.ENG4.W.02] Evidence: A survey found seniors lacked quiet work time after school. Which reasoning best supports later library hours?
{
~A. Quiet work time is unrelated to applications.#Incorrect. It can directly affect application completion.
~B. The survey proves every student needs the same schedule.#Incorrect. This overstates the evidence.
=C. Later library hours could provide a structured space for students who lack quiet time elsewhere.#Correct. This explains how evidence supports the claim.
~D. Libraries should close earlier.#Incorrect. This does not follow from the evidence.
}

::ENG4_U04_UA_Q10::
Question ID: ENG4_U04_UA_Q10
[MLA.ENG4.W.03] Passage: "Numbers showed the storm's size. The sentences showed where it had touched a life." Which analysis is strongest?
{
=A. The contrast shows that data can measure a storm while personal records reveal human impact.#Correct. This explains function and meaning.
~B. The passage says numbers exist.#Incorrect. This is too literal.
~C. The passage rejects all data.#Incorrect. It says the point was useful.
~D. The passage is only about handwriting.#Incorrect. The contrast is broader.
}

::ENG4_U04_UA_Q11::
Question ID: ENG4_U04_UA_Q11
[MLA.ENG4.W.04] Which revision strengthens argument elaboration?
{
~A. This evidence is good because it is evidence.#Incorrect. This is circular.
~B. Evidence is in the paragraph.#Incorrect. This does not develop reasoning.
~C. The claim is good.#Incorrect. This is vague.
=D. This evidence matters because it shows students need applied practice, not only definitions.#Correct. This explains significance.
}

::ENG4_U04_UA_Q12::
Question ID: ENG4_U04_UA_Q12
[MLA.ENG4.L.01] Which sentence avoids a comma splice?
{
~A. The sources show benefits, they also show challenges.#Incorrect. This is a comma splice.
=B. The sources show benefits, but they also identify practical challenges.#Correct. The conjunction correctly joins the clauses.
~C. The sources show benefits, challenges.#Incorrect. This is unclear.
~D. The sources, benefits and challenges.#Incorrect. This is fragmented.
}

::ENG4_U04_UA_Q13::
Question ID: ENG4_U04_UA_Q13
[MLA.ENG4.RS.01] What should happen before writing a synthesis statement?
{
~A. Ignore sources that complicate the claim.#Incorrect. Complications can improve synthesis.
=B. Understand and paraphrase each source accurately.#Correct. Accurate understanding comes before synthesis.
~C. Choose an opinion before reading.#Incorrect. Evidence should guide the claim.
~D. Copy sources without understanding.#Incorrect. Copying is not synthesis.
}

::ENG4_U04_UA_Q14::
Question ID: ENG4_U04_UA_Q14
[MLA.ENG4.W.02] Which claim is too broad for one city pilot program?
{
~A. The pilot suggests placement affects station use.#Incorrect. This is appropriately limited.
~B. The city should review placement before expansion.#Incorrect. This follows from the pilot.
=C. Every city in the world must build stations immediately.#Correct. This overstates what one pilot can support.
~D. Stations near transit stops may be useful.#Incorrect. This is limited and evidence-based.
}

::ENG4_U04_UA_Q15::
Question ID: ENG4_U04_UA_Q15
[MLA.ENG4.W.03] Which answer is analysis rather than summary?
{
=A. Mason's revision of the ramp shows that responsible design must serve real people, not only appear impressive.#Correct. This explains author choice and meaning.
~B. Mason works alone in a workshop.#Incorrect. This summarizes.
~C. The parking lot lights turn on.#Incorrect. This identifies a detail.
~D. The plans are on a table.#Incorrect. This summarizes setting.
}

::ENG4_U04_UA_Q16::
Question ID: ENG4_U04_UA_Q16
[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Which paraphrase preserves meaning?
{
~A. Original: Response times sometimes slowed. Paraphrase: Response times always stopped completely.#Incorrect. This exaggerates.
~B. Original: Response times sometimes slowed. Paraphrase: Communication became perfect.#Incorrect. This reverses the source.
~C. Original: Response times sometimes slowed. Paraphrase: No one communicated.#Incorrect. This overstates the source.
=D. Original: Response times sometimes slowed. Paraphrase: Communication was occasionally delayed.#Correct. This preserves meaning.
}

::ENG4_U04_UA_Q17::
Question ID: ENG4_U04_UA_Q17
[MLA.ENG4.W.03] Which digital communication practice is strongest?
{
~A. Send unclear messages with no context.#Incorrect. This weakens communication.
~B. Use only emojis for a formal checkpoint.#Incorrect. This is not appropriate for the task.
=C. Use a clear subject, concise explanation, evidence when needed, and a professional tone.#Correct. This supports audience and purpose.
~D. Leave the main point unstated.#Incorrect. Clear communication states the point.
}

::ENG4_U04_UA_Q18::
Question ID: ENG4_U04_UA_Q18
[MLA.ENG4.W.04] Which revision improves analysis?
{
=A. The author uses the open door and marked drafts to show that revision continues beyond the workshop session.#Correct. This explains detail and function.
~B. The passage has details and stuff.#Incorrect. This is vague.
~C. The passage is about a room.#Incorrect. This is summary.
~D. It is interesting.#Incorrect. This is personal reaction, not analysis.
}

::ENG4_U04_UA_Q19::
Question ID: ENG4_U04_UA_Q19
[MLA.ENG4.W.02] Which sentence best explains consequence?
{
~A. The source was a survey.#Incorrect. This is context, not consequence.
=B. Without applied practice, students may face adult decisions without knowing how to compare real costs.#Correct. This explains a possible result.
~C. The word credit has six letters.#Incorrect. This is irrelevant.
~D. Financial literacy is a phrase.#Incorrect. This does not explain consequence.
}

::ENG4_U04_UA_Q20::
Question ID: ENG4_U04_UA_Q20
[MLA.ENG4.W.03] What makes an analytical claim strong?
{
~A. It only retells events.#Incorrect. That is summary.
~B. It avoids evidence.#Incorrect. Claims need evidence.
~C. It uses vague words.#Incorrect. Precision matters.
=D. It names a text choice and explains its meaning or effect.#Correct. This is the core of analysis.
}

::ENG4_U04_UA_Q21::
Question ID: ENG4_U04_UA_Q21
[MLA.ENG4.RS.01] Which source relationship supports a qualified claim?
{
=A. Two sources show benefits, while one identifies a condition for success.#Correct. This supports a claim with limits.
~B. All sources are unrelated.#Incorrect. Unrelated sources cannot support synthesis.
~C. The writer ignores complications.#Incorrect. Ignoring complications weakens research.
~D. One source is copied without understanding.#Incorrect. Copying is not synthesis.
}

::ENG4_U04_UA_Q22::
Question ID: ENG4_U04_UA_Q22
[MLA.ENG4.W.02] Which option is reasoning rather than evidence?
{
~A. A survey found students lacked quiet work time.#Incorrect. This is evidence.
~B. A report listed application completion rates.#Incorrect. This is evidence.
=C. This evidence matters because quiet work time affects students' ability to complete detailed applications.#Correct. This explains evidence.
~D. A source stated a staffing concern.#Incorrect. This is evidence.
}

::ENG4_U04_UA_Q23::
Question ID: ENG4_U04_UA_Q23
[MLA.ENG4.L.01] Which sentence uses precise academic language?
{
~A. The stuff shows things.#Incorrect. This is vague.
~B. It is good because good.#Incorrect. This is circular.
~C. There are many ideas or whatever.#Incorrect. This is informal.
=D. The evidence reveals a practical gap between knowing terms and applying them to decisions.#Correct. This uses precise academic language.
}

::ENG4_U04_UA_Q24::
Question ID: ENG4_U04_UA_Q24
[MLA.ENG4.W.04] Which revision question is most useful?
{
~A. Can I make the sentence more vague?#Incorrect. Revision should improve precision.
=B. Does my explanation show why the evidence matters?#Correct. This checks development.
~C. Can I delete all evidence?#Incorrect. Evidence is required.
~D. Can I avoid the task?#Incorrect. Revision should address the task.
}

::ENG4_U04_UA_Q25::
Question ID: ENG4_U04_UA_Q25
[MLA.ENG4.W.03] Which statement best distinguishes summary from analysis?
{
=A. Summary says what happens; analysis explains how author choices create meaning.#Correct. This clearly distinguishes the two.
~B. Summary and analysis are identical.#Incorrect. They serve different purposes.
~C. Analysis avoids the text.#Incorrect. Analysis uses text evidence.
~D. Summary explains author choices in depth.#Incorrect. That describes analysis.
}

::ENG4_U04_UA_Q26::
Question ID: ENG4_U04_UA_Q26
[MLA.ENG4.RS.01] Topic: Artificial intelligence in schools. Which research question is most focused and researchable?
{
~A. Is AI good or bad?#Incorrect. This answer does not fully match the lesson skill or it misreads the evidence, research focus, or source information in the item.
~B. Why is technology everywhere?#Incorrect. This answer does not fully match the lesson skill or it misreads the evidence, research focus, or source information in the item.
~C. Should everyone stop using computers?#Incorrect. This answer does not fully match the lesson skill or it misreads the evidence, research focus, or source information in the item.
=D. How do AI brainstorming tools affect student revision habits in senior English classes?#Correct. This answer applies the lesson skill accurately and matches the evidence, research focus, or source information in the item.
}

::ENG4_U04_UA_Q27::
Question ID: ENG4_U04_UA_Q27
[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Source note: A report says bus delays increased most often at afternoon transfer stations. Which paraphrase is accurate?
{
~A. The report proved all buses are late every afternoon.#Incorrect. This answer does not fully match the lesson skill or it misreads the evidence, research focus, or source information in the item.
~B. The report said morning routes never have delays.#Incorrect. This answer does not fully match the lesson skill or it misreads the evidence, research focus, or source information in the item.
=C. The report found that afternoon transfer stations had the most frequent bus delay increases.#Correct. This answer applies the lesson skill accurately and matches the evidence, research focus, or source information in the item.
~D. The report was about school lunches.#Incorrect. This answer does not fully match the lesson skill or it misreads the evidence, research focus, or source information in the item.
}

::ENG4_U04_UA_Q28::
Question ID: ENG4_U04_UA_Q28
[MLA.ENG4.V.01] In research, what does scope mean?
{
~A. The emotional tone of a poem.#Incorrect. This answer does not fully match the lesson skill or it misreads the evidence, research focus, or source information in the item.
=B. The boundaries or size of a research question.#Correct. This answer applies the lesson skill accurately and matches the evidence, research focus, or source information in the item.
~C. The number of paragraphs in an essay.#Incorrect. This answer does not fully match the lesson skill or it misreads the evidence, research focus, or source information in the item.
~D. The final sentence of a source.#Incorrect. This answer does not fully match the lesson skill or it misreads the evidence, research focus, or source information in the item.
}

::ENG4_U04_UA_Q29::
Question ID: ENG4_U04_UA_Q29
[MLA.ENG4.RS.01] Research question: How do evening library hours affect adult learners? Source A gives attendance data. Source B gives an interview with one learner. Source C gives staffing cost information. Which source combination is strongest for an initial synthesis?
{
=A. Sources A, B, and C together because they show demand, human impact, and feasibility.#Correct. This answer applies the lesson skill accurately and matches the evidence, research focus, or source information in the item.
~B. Source B alone because personal stories are always enough.#Incorrect. This answer does not fully match the lesson skill or it misreads the evidence, research focus, or source information in the item.
~C. Source C alone because cost is the only issue.#Incorrect. This answer does not fully match the lesson skill or it misreads the evidence, research focus, or source information in the item.
~D. None of the sources because they are not identical.#Incorrect. This answer does not fully match the lesson skill or it misreads the evidence, research focus, or source information in the item.
}

::ENG4_U04_UA_Q30::
Question ID: ENG4_U04_UA_Q30
[MLA.ENG4.R.06] Source A says evening lab attendance increased 28 percent. How does this support the central idea that evening hours help adult learners?
{
~A. It proves every adult learner succeeded in every course.#Incorrect. This answer does not fully match the lesson skill or it misreads the evidence, research focus, or source information in the item.
~B. It shows that libraries should close earlier.#Incorrect. This answer does not fully match the lesson skill or it misreads the evidence, research focus, or source information in the item.
=C. It provides measurable evidence that more people used the service during evening access.#Correct. This answer applies the lesson skill accurately and matches the evidence, research focus, or source information in the item.
~D. It has no connection to the central idea.#Incorrect. This answer does not fully match the lesson skill or it misreads the evidence, research focus, or source information in the item.
}

::ENG4_U04_UA_Q31::
Question ID: ENG4_U04_UA_Q31
[MLA.ENG4.RS.01] Source notes: A survey says flexible schedules reduce interruptions. A report says unclear communication can slow teams. A study says shared calendars improve results. Which synthesis is strongest?
{
~A. Flexible schedules are always better than office schedules.#Incorrect. This overstates the sources and ignores the communication concern.
=B. Flexible schedules can support focused work when teams also use clear communication systems.#Correct. This connects the benefit, the concern, and the condition shown across the sources.
~C. Shared calendars are the only topic mentioned in the sources.#Incorrect. This focuses on one detail and misses the larger relationship among the sources.
~D. The sources prove communication is impossible with flexible schedules.#Incorrect. This distorts the evidence by turning a concern into an absolute claim.
}

::ENG4_U04_UA_Q32::
Question ID: ENG4_U04_UA_Q32
[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Source note: A city health brief reports that shaded seating can reduce heat stress for older adults and transit riders. Which paraphrase is accurate?
{
~A. The brief says shaded seating eliminates all health risks in the city.#Incorrect. This exaggerates the source beyond what it states.
~B. The brief argues that only older adults should use public seating.#Incorrect. This adds a restriction that is not in the source.
~C. The brief is mainly about shopping habits downtown.#Incorrect. This shifts the focus away from the health information in the source.
=D. A health brief says shaded seating may lower heat stress for older adults and transit riders.#Correct. This preserves the meaning without copying the wording or adding unsupported claims.
}

::ENG4_U04_UA_Q33::
Question ID: ENG4_U04_UA_Q33
[MLA.ENG4.W.02] Issue: Later library hours for seniors. Which claim is most precise and evidence-based?
{
~A. Libraries should stay open forever because students like libraries.#Incorrect. This is exaggerated and not tied to the evidence.
~B. Seniors should never complete applications at school.#Incorrect. This ignores evidence that structured school access can help students.
~C. Application season is stressful for everyone.#Incorrect. This is a general observation, not an arguable claim.
=D. Schools should offer limited later library hours during application season if supervision and safety procedures are planned.#Correct. This claim is specific, arguable, and aligned to the source conditions.
}

::ENG4_U04_UA_Q34::
Question ID: ENG4_U04_UA_Q34
[MLA.ENG4.R.08] Evidence: A pilot program found that reusable water stations reduced plastic waste when placed near entrances and food areas. Which reasoning is strongest?
{
~A. The evidence proves every event has the same layout.#Incorrect. This overgeneralizes beyond the evidence.
~B. The evidence means plastic waste is no longer a public concern.#Incorrect. This conclusion is too broad and unsupported.
=C. The evidence supports placement rules because the success of the stations depended partly on where people could access them.#Correct. This explains how the evidence supports a practical policy design.
~D. The evidence is irrelevant because entrances are not part of events.#Incorrect. This misreads the practical detail in the evidence.
}

::ENG4_U04_UA_Q35::
Question ID: ENG4_U04_UA_Q35
[MLA.ENG4.W.02] Evidence: Seniors could define credit but struggled to compare repayment timelines. Which elaboration is strongest?
{
=A. This evidence shows that students may know terms but still need practice applying those terms to real financial decisions.#Correct. This explains the significance of the evidence.
~B. This proves financial literacy is important because it is important.#Incorrect. This repeats the idea without explaining the evidence.
~C. This evidence means every senior will make poor financial choices.#Incorrect. This overstates what the evidence proves.
~D. This evidence is about school lunch decisions.#Incorrect. This misreads the source.
}

::ENG4_U04_UA_Q36::
Question ID: ENG4_U04_UA_Q36
[MLA.ENG4.R.06] Which paragraph order best develops an argument?
{
~A. Consequence, random detail, claim, unrelated example.#Incorrect. This order lacks logical structure.
=B. Claim, evidence, context, significance, consequence.#Correct. This order guides the reader from position to developed explanation.
~C. Evidence, evidence, evidence, no explanation.#Incorrect. Evidence needs elaboration.
~D. Conclusion, title, question, unrelated fact.#Incorrect. This does not develop an argument.
}

::ENG4_U04_UA_Q37::
Question ID: ENG4_U04_UA_Q37
[MLA.ENG4.W.03] Passage: "The room emptied quickly after the vote... She did not call the meeting a failure. Not yet." Which response is analysis rather than summary?
{
=A. The passage uses the quiet aftermath of the vote to show Maya interpreting failure as a chance to revise.#Correct. This explains how the passage creates meaning.
~B. Maya stayed after a meeting and folded papers.#Incorrect. This only retells what happened.
~C. The passage includes a room, folders, and lights.#Incorrect. This lists details without explaining function.
~D. Maya was in a hallway after a vote.#Incorrect. This summarizes events.
}

::ENG4_U04_UA_Q38::
Question ID: ENG4_U04_UA_Q38
[MLA.ENG4.R.05] What is the function of the short sentence "Not yet"?
{
~A. It proves Maya has no opinion about the vote.#Incorrect. The sentence reveals her developing interpretation.
~B. It changes the setting to a different city.#Incorrect. The sentence does not change setting.
~C. It removes the passage's reflective mood.#Incorrect. It strengthens the reflective mood.
=D. It creates a restrained tone of possibility after disappointment.#Correct. The short sentence shifts the moment from failure toward persistence.
}

::ENG4_U04_UA_Q39::
Question ID: ENG4_U04_UA_Q39
[MLA.ENG4.W.03] Passage: A paper coastline map and a digital risk model are shown together. Which claim is most analytical?
{
~A. The room has maps and a screen.#Incorrect. This summarizes setting without interpreting meaning.
~B. Elena is standing in a room.#Incorrect. This is a literal detail, not analysis.
=C. The contrast between paper and digital maps shows that future planning depends on understanding the past.#Correct. This interprets the function of the contrast.
~D. The passage includes blue lines.#Incorrect. This identifies a detail without explaining function.
}

::ENG4_U04_UA_Q40::
Question ID: ENG4_U04_UA_Q40
[MLA.ENG4.W.02] Which evidence best supports the claim that the passage contrasts memory and prediction?
{
~A. "The map room was the quietest place in the building."#Incorrect. This establishes setting but less directly supports the contrast.
=B. "The future... was not a clean replacement for the past. It was an argument with it."#Correct. This line directly supports the interpretation.
~C. "Elena traced one curve with her finger."#Incorrect. This is useful detail but not the strongest evidence for the claim.
~D. "The new digital model glowed."#Incorrect. This supports only one side of the contrast.
}
